### Role

For the Cornell Design Build Fly team I was responsible for choosing the airfoil and creating the wing CAD for our 2025 aircraft.

### Airfoil selection

Because of fuselage geometry and manufacturing constraints we went with a rectangular high wing.  
Using xFLR5 I analyzed more than 30 candidate airfoils under our expected flight conditions and servo limits.  
The FX 60-126 provided the best mix of lift, drag, and control authority, so it became the basis for the final wing.

### Wing design and integration

To connect the wing to the fuselage, I designed a 3D-printed center section that keys into the fuselage and carries the main loads.  
The rest of the 3.5-foot span uses hardwood spars and balsa ribs, which keeps the structure light while staying easy to manufacture and repair.

I modeled the full wing in SOLIDWORKS, building one half and then mirroring it to create the complete assembly that plugs into the overall aircraft CAD.

**Technologies used:** xFLR5, SOLIDWORKS, 3D printing, hardwood and balsa construction
